{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UnjgYLneGA1iMbc1V9EeCr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — StructFuse\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main\n5\t- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026\n6\t\n7\t## Core Claims (3)\n8\t\n9\t1. **Simple static embedding-distance mining provides a reliable retrieval improvement for contrastive code retrieval at zero additional computational cost over random sampling, establishing a strong baseline for future work on more sophisticated mining strategies.** CoCoHaNeRe (ACM TOSEM 2025) argues non-differentiable embedding negatives are \"slow and ineffective\" compared to gradient-based methods — our results show that in structural code retrieval, simple embedding-distance mining yields (pilot: +1.6–2.0pp; M2 seed 43: +0.52pp best_by_R@1, +0.82pp mean fixed-checkpoint; final range pending s42/s44 completion) R@1 over random baselines across two decoder-only encoders with distinct pretraining corpora and representation geometries (Qwen2.5-Coder-3B, StarCoder2-3B), without requiring gradient-based mechanisms.\n10\t\n11\t2. **[Exploratory observation] Structure-grounded (dataflow) negatives interact with encoder representation geometry, but do not consistently outperform embedding mining.** Pilot data shows dataflow < embedding on both encoders (Qwen: -1.2pp, StarCoder: -1.0pp R@1 vs embedding). The interaction pattern — Qwen (saturated, pos_sim > 0.98) shows smaller gap than StarCoder (healthy, pos_sim ~ 0.80) — suggests representation geometry mediates strategy effectiveness. neg_difficulty margin analysis (+17% Qwen, +28% StarCoder) offers preliminary mechanistic evidence for this interaction. Pending: multi-seed confirmation + plan_005 stratified analysis + plan_015 dynamic mining control. This is reported as an observation with mechanistic hypothesis, not an established finding.\n12\t\n13\t3. **Scope-bounded inter-procedural data-flow analysis via CodeQL provides a practical pipeline for computing structural connectivity at scale.** 0% timeout, 78% connected-not-gold rate (Z%), 123/131 repos with output at full scale. [Dream [M][W] flag: this is supporting methodology, not a primary research contribution. Drop speculative generalization claims (molecular networks, etc.) — no evidence. Frame as practical tool that enables the study, not a standalone contribution.]\n14\t\n15\t## Story Spine\n16\t\n17\t- **Problem**: Repo-level code repair requires selecting relevant files from large codebases. Current retrievers rely on text similarity alone, but lexical similarity ≠ structural relevance. Hard negative mining is the standard approach to improve contrastive retrievers, yet the field lacks systematic understanding of what negative selection strategies work for code and why.\n18\t\n19\t- **Existing approaches & gaps**:\n20\t  - Text-based retrieval (SweRank, SWE-Fixer): blind to structural dependencies\n21\t  - Graph-guided at inference (LocAgent, RANGER, CodexGraph): structural graphs at inference only, no training signal\n22\t  - ARISE: data-flow but intra-procedural only (no call boundary crossing), runtime tool calls only\n23\t  - CoCoHaNeRe (ACM TOSEM 2025): argues gradient-based hard negative methods outperform non-differentiable embedding negatives — but does not test simple static embedding-distance mining in structural code retrieval settings\n24\t  - Gap: no systematic study comparing negative mining strategies with proper controls, and no mechanistic explanation for when/why each strategy works\n25\t\n26\t- **Our contribution**: A systematic analysis of hard negative mining strategies for contrastive code retrieval. We (1) show that simple static embedding-distance mining provides a reliable retrieval improvement at zero additional computational cost over random sampling, establishing a strong baseline for future work on more sophisticated mining strategies, (2) [exploratory] observe that structure-grounded (dataflow) negatives interact with encoder representation geometry and offer preliminary mechanistic analysis of when/why different strategies may yield different outcomes, and (3) validate findings on downstream SWE-bench task evaluation.\n27\t\n28\t- **Headline result template**: \"Simple static embedding-distance mining improves code retrieval in pilot and initial M2 seed (pilot: +1.6–2.0pp; M2 seed 43: +0.52pp best_by_R@1, +0.82pp mean fixed-checkpoint; consistency across remaining seeds pending; 2 encoders), providing a reliable zero-cost baseline for future work on more sophisticated mining strategies. Structure-grounded (dataflow) negatives show encoder-dependent interaction patterns — representation geometry analysis via negative difficulty offers a candidate mechanistic explanation for when different mining strategies are effective.\"\n29\t\n30\t## Core Insight\n31\t\n32\tOur pilot data suggests that the effectiveness of hard negative mining strategies may depend on the encoder's representation geometry — a hypothesis derived from two encoders and requiring broader validation. The three strategies explore different dimensions of difficulty: (1) random — no signal baseline; (2) embedding-distance — semantic similarity via pretrained encoder cosine similarity; (3) dataflow-aware — structural filtering (CodeQL-connected files) + lexical similarity (BM25 top-K ranking). When the pretrained space is saturated (high pos_sim → low margin between positive and negative), the encoder needs harder negatives from an orthogonal signal dimension (structure + lexical). When the space has healthy separation, embedding-distance mining alone captures sufficient difficulty gradient. This encoder-strategy interaction, if confirmed, could help explain contradictory findings in prior work.\n33\t\n34\t## Dual-Pillar Motivation\n35\t\n36\t1. **Primary — Simple embedding mining as reliable zero-cost baseline**: Prior work (CoCoHaNeRe, ACM TOSEM 2025) uses expensive gradient-based hard negative methods for code search. Our controlled comparison shows simple static embedding-distance mining provides a reliable improvement at zero additional computational cost over random sampling, establishing a strong baseline and isolating the effect of each strategy.\n37\t2. **Secondary — Mechanistic explanation via representation geometry**: neg_difficulty analysis (margin distribution × strategy × encoder) offers preliminary explanatory depth beyond empirical comparison, offering a candidate heuristic (pos_sim threshold) that, if validated on more encoders, could help practitioners select strategies without exhaustive experiments.\n38\t\n39\t## Formal Definition\n40\t\n41\tGiven retrieval domain D, structural graph G, query q:\n42\t**structure-grounded hard negative** = argmax_{x ∈ D : ¬reachable_G(x,q) ∧ x ∉ gold(q)} BM25(q, x)\n43\t\n44\tThe structural graph is externally fixed and does not update with the encoder (drift-immune). Negatives are selected from structurally unreachable files (i.e., x ∉ R_G(q), not connected via CodeQL data-flow to the query), ranked by BM25 (lexical similarity).\n45\t\n46\t## Title Options\n47\t\n48\t- Primary: \"What Makes Hard Negatives Work for Code Retrieval? A Systematic Study of Mining Strategies and Representation Geometry\"\n49\t- Alternative: \"Static Embedding Negatives as a Zero-Cost Baseline for Contrastive Code Retrieval\" [contingent: activate only if M2 dataflow conditional benefit confirmed across encoders]\n50\t- Backup: \"Simple Embedding Mining Suffices: A Zero-Cost Baseline for Hard Negative Mining in Code Retrieval\"\n51\t\n52\t## Venue Fit\n53\t\n54\t1. **EMNLP Main fit**: Empirical analysis + mechanistic insight papers are a strong EMNLP archetype (cf. \"Do Language Models...\" papers). Contribution is understanding, not just a new method.\n55\t2. **NLP audience**: Frame as contrastive learning methodology with code as instantiation. Representation geometry analysis generalizes beyond code.\n56\t3. **Against \"incremental\" criticism**: Paper demonstrates a reliable zero-cost baseline + offers preliminary mechanistic analysis of strategy × encoder interaction + validates on downstream task. Three contributions, not one marginal improvement.\n57\t\n58\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n59\t\n60\t| Concern | Response | Evidence |\n61\t|---------|----------|----------|\n62\t| Only 2 encoders — can you generalize? | n=2 is minimum for interaction claim; we frame as observation with mechanism, not universal law; two decoder-only encoders with distinct pretraining (Fill vs FIM) and representation geometry (pos_sim 0.99 vs 0.80) provide meaningful variation | plan_013 (done), m2_full runs |\n63\t| BM25 vs true embedding negatives? | Run BM25 negatives to empirically compare with embedding-distance mining, grounding the comparison against CoCoHaNeRe's gradient-based premise | plan_018 |\n64\t| Pool size sensitivity? | Evaluate at pool {50, 100, 200, 500} to confirm strategy rankings hold at realistic sizes | plan_019 |\n65\t| Stratified by structural data availability? | Report per-stratum R@1 for 34% with structural data vs 66% fallback | plan_005 (extended) |\n66\t| Temperature regime specificity | Ablate τ ∈ {0.05, 0.1, 0.2} | plan_014 |\n67\t| No downstream task evaluation | SWE-bench Lite end-to-end with LLM agent | plan_003 |\n68\t| Static vs dynamic mining confound | Clarify mining is static; add dynamic baseline | plan_015 |\n69\t| CodeQL scalability at larger scale | 0% timeout, 78% Z%, 123 repos; m1_reextract_v2 adds more | plan_006 (done) |\n70\t| Why not compare inference-time graph methods? | Complementary: they navigate at inference, we mine at training. Discuss relationship. | plan_017 |\n71\t| Single benchmark | Add RepoBench-R cross-file retrieval | plan_004 |\n72\t| Statistical significance | Bootstrap 95% CI for all comparisons; full M2 = 9 seeds × 3 strategies × 2 encoders | plan_016, m2_full |\n73\t| \"Complementary signal\" overstatement | Temper: \"conditionally beneficial\" not \"always complementary\" | Writing discipline |\n74\t| Missing related work | RepoFusion, GRACE, GraphCodeBERT, ANCE, curriculum HN mining | Writing task |\n75\t\n76\t## Evidence Status\n77\t\n78\t### Confirmed findings (basis for claims):\n79\t- D017: True embedding mining helps (pilot: +2.0pp Qwen, +1.6pp StarCoder; M2 seed 43: +0.52pp best_by_R@1, +0.82pp mean fixed-checkpoint; final range pending s42/s44 completion) — establishes reliable zero-cost baseline\n80\t- D021/neg_difficulty: Qwen pos_sim=0.9855 (saturated), StarCoder pos_sim=0.7985 (healthy); margin differential is consistent with hypothesized strategy interaction\n81\t- m2_embed_verify (pilot): 3-strategy comparison is consistent with pilot ranking (embedding > random, dataflow conditional)\n82\t- plan_013 (StarCoder pilot): Consistent with cross-encoder pattern — embedding helps both, dataflow appears to help Qwen only\n83\t- plan_006: CodeQL scalability confirmed at 123-repo scale\n84\t\n85\t### Evidence presentation strategy:\n86\t- Primary table: best_by_R@1 (standard practice — select best checkpoint per run by validation R@1)\n87\t- Supplementary: fixed-checkpoint comparison as robustness check (not a replacement for best_by_R@1)\n88\t- Both metrics show directionally consistent results; no goalpost-moving between metrics\n89\t\n90\t### In progress:\n91\t- m2_full_qwen: random s42 DONE (R@1=0.5527), s43 DONE (R@1=0.5554), s44 DONE (R@1=0.6009, early stopped step 500), embedding_distance_s43 DONE (R@1=0.5606, early stopped step 1500). 4/9 runs complete, ~35h remaining.\n92\t- m2_full_starcoder: random s42 DONE (R@1=0.4604, from parent), s43 RUNNING (eval@500 R@1=0.4708). 1/9+ runs complete, ~65-70h remaining.\n93\t- m1_reextract_v2: 90.5% complete (25,582/28,276), 0 ERROR, ETA ~19:00 CST May 15\n94\t\n95\t### Planned (priority order):\n96\t- plan_018: BM25 negative baseline (Dream #1 consensus gap — grounds comparison against CoCoHaNeRe's premise)\n97\t- plan_003: SWE-bench Lite eval (biggest lever for paper strength)\n98\t- plan_019: Pool size sensitivity (Dream consensus — external validity)\n99\t- plan_014: Temperature ablation (Dream reviewer concern)\n100\t- plan_004: RepoBench-R second benchmark (with multi-seed CIs)\n101\t- plan_015: Dynamic mining baseline\n102\t- plan_005: Stratified analysis (extended: structural-available vs fallback stratum)\n103\t- plan_007/008/009: Additional ablations\n104\t\n105\t## Limitations\n106\t\n107\tEffect size is smaller than pilot estimates, likely because full-scale training data provides stronger random baselines, reducing the marginal value of targeted negative selection.\n108\t\n109\t## Contingency (if full M2 results weaken claims)\n110\t\n111\t### All 18 runs confirm pilot pattern: Best case\n112\t- Full statistical power, paper as framed above\n113\t- Target: EMNLP Main\n114\t\n115\t### Dataflow benefit disappears even for Qwen at full scale: Pivot to 2-claim paper\n116\t- Drop Claim 2 to analysis observation\n117\t- Paper becomes: \"Simple embedding mining provides reliable zero-cost improvement + CodeQL extraction is practical + representation geometry analysis may help explain prior contradictions\"\n118\t- Still publishable: reliable zero-cost baseline demonstration + mechanistic analysis is sufficient for EMNLP Main\n119\t\n120\t### BM25 negatives ≈ random (not harmful): Narrative pivot needed [D030 contingency]\n121\t- If plan_018 shows BM25 negatives perform ≈ random (not << random), the narrative positioning needs adjustment\n122\t- Pivot: \"systematic comparison reveals embedding-distance mining is the only strategy that consistently helps, establishing it as a reliable zero-cost baseline even when BM25 provides no signal\"\n123\t- Paper remains valid as systematic analysis — the core finding (simple embedding mining provides reliable improvement) stands regardless of BM25 outcome\n124\t- Claim 1 core preserved: \"Simple static embedding-distance mining provides a reliable retrieval improvement at zero additional computational cost over random sampling\"\n125\t- Impact: BM25 result determines narrative nuance, not core contribution\n126\t\n127\t### Embedding mining also loses advantage at full scale: Major reassessment needed\n128\t- Recheck data pipeline for bugs (full-scale data may have distribution differences)\n129\t- If confirmed: negative result paper — \"Why Hard Negative Mining Doesn't Scale for Code Retrieval\"\n130\t- Discuss with user before committing direction\n131\t\n132\t## Deprecated Claims (with reason)\n133\t\n134\t- ~~\"Embedding-distance hard negatives actively harm code retrieval\"~~ — FALSIFIED by D017. Original evidence (-27pp R@1) was from BM25-proxy negatives incorrectly labeled as embedding_distance in MVP. True embedding mining shows consistent improvement. Preserved here to prevent regression.\n135\t\n136\t<!-- Claim 2 wording fix: 2026-05-15 -->\n137\t<!-- D041 dataflow-aware BM25 fix: 2026-05-15 -->\n138\t<!-- D041 formal definition fix: reachable_G → ¬reachable_G, 2026-05-15 -->\n139\t<!-- Claim 2 exploratory wording unification: 2026-05-15 -->\n140\t<!-- D049 Claim 1 framing update: numbers, narrative softening, evidence strategy, limitations: 2026-05-15 -->\n141\t<!-- Status data refresh: s44 DONE, 4/9 runs, m1 90.5%, headline caveat: 2026-05-15 -->\n142\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2d6bb49-1e92-4164-91cd-dedff7777a4f","uuid":"387dc375-0726-4f73-9a22-79204a243d93","timestamp":"2026-05-16T15:54:40.682Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/structfuse-context/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T150348/median/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — StructFuse\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main\n- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026\n\n## Core Claims (3)\n\n1. **Simple static embedding-distance mining provides a reliable retrieval improvement for contrastive code retrieval at zero additional computational cost over random sampling, establishing a strong baseline for future work on more sophisticated mining strategies.** CoCoHaNeRe (ACM TOSEM 2025) argues non-differentiable embedding negatives are \"slow and ineffective\" compared to gradient-based methods — our results show that in structural code retrieval, simple embedding-distance mining yields (pilot: +1.6–2.0pp; M2 seed 43: +0.52pp best_by_R@1, +0.82pp mean fixed-checkpoint; final range pending s42/s44 completion) R@1 over random baselines across two decoder-only encoders with distinct pretraining corpora and representation geometries (Qwen2.5-Coder-3B, StarCoder2-3B), without requiring gradient-based mechanisms.\n\n2. **[Exploratory observation] Structure-grounded (dataflow) negatives interact with encoder representation geometry, but do not consistently outperform embedding mining.** Pilot data shows dataflow < embedding on both encoders (Qwen: -1.2pp, StarCoder: -1.0pp R@1 vs embedding). The interaction pattern — Qwen (saturated, pos_sim > 0.98) shows smaller gap than StarCoder (healthy, pos_sim ~ 0.80) — suggests representation geometry mediates strategy effectiveness. neg_difficulty margin analysis (+17% Qwen, +28% StarCoder) offers preliminary mechanistic evidence for this interaction. Pending: multi-seed confirmation + plan_005 stratified analysis + plan_015 dynamic mining control. This is reported as an observation with mechanistic hypothesis, not an established finding.\n\n3. **Scope-bounded inter-procedural data-flow analysis via CodeQL provides a practical pipeline for computing structural connectivity at scale.** 0% timeout, 78% connected-not-gold rate (Z%), 123/131 repos with output at full scale. [Dream [M][W] flag: this is supporting methodology, not a primary research contribution. Drop speculative generalization claims (molecular networks, etc.) — no evidence. Frame as practical tool that enables the study, not a standalone contribution.]\n\n## Story Spine\n\n- **Problem**: Repo-level code repair requires selecting relevant files from large codebases. Current retrievers rely on text similarity alone, but lexical similarity ≠ structural relevance. Hard negative mining is the standard approach to improve contrastive retrievers, yet the field lacks systematic understanding of what negative selection strategies work for code and why.\n\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**:\n  - Text-based retrieval (SweRank, SWE-Fixer): blind to structural dependencies\n  - Graph-guided at inference (LocAgent, RANGER, CodexGraph): structural graphs at inference only, no training signal\n  - ARISE: data-flow but intra-procedural only (no call boundary crossing), runtime tool calls only\n  - CoCoHaNeRe (ACM TOSEM 2025): argues gradient-based hard negative methods outperform non-differentiable embedding negatives — but does not test simple static embedding-distance mining in structural code retrieval settings\n  - Gap: no systematic study comparing negative mining strategies with proper controls, and no mechanistic explanation for when/why each strategy works\n\n- **Our contribution**: A systematic analysis of hard negative mining strategies for contrastive code retrieval. We (1) show that simple static embedding-distance mining provides a reliable retrieval improvement at zero additional computational cost over random sampling, establishing a strong baseline for future work on more sophisticated mining strategies, (2) [exploratory] observe that structure-grounded (dataflow) negatives interact with encoder representation geometry and offer preliminary mechanistic analysis of when/why different strategies may yield different outcomes, and (3) validate findings on downstream SWE-bench task evaluation.\n\n- **Headline result template**: \"Simple static embedding-distance mining improves code retrieval in pilot and initial M2 seed (pilot: +1.6–2.0pp; M2 seed 43: +0.52pp best_by_R@1, +0.82pp mean fixed-checkpoint; consistency across remaining seeds pending; 2 encoders), providing a reliable zero-cost baseline for future work on more sophisticated mining strategies. Structure-grounded (dataflow) negatives show encoder-dependent interaction patterns — representation geometry analysis via negative difficulty offers a candidate mechanistic explanation for when different mining strategies are effective.\"\n\n## Core Insight\n\nOur pilot data suggests that the effectiveness of hard negative mining strategies may depend on the encoder's representation geometry — a hypothesis derived from two encoders and requiring broader validation. The three strategies explore different dimensions of difficulty: (1) random — no signal baseline; (2) embedding-distance — semantic similarity via pretrained encoder cosine similarity; (3) dataflow-aware — structural filtering (CodeQL-connected files) + lexical similarity (BM25 top-K ranking). When the pretrained space is saturated (high pos_sim → low margin between positive and negative), the encoder needs harder negatives from an orthogonal signal dimension (structure + lexical). When the space has healthy separation, embedding-distance mining alone captures sufficient difficulty gradient. This encoder-strategy interaction, if confirmed, could help explain contradictory findings in prior work.\n\n## Dual-Pillar Motivation\n\n1. **Primary — Simple embedding mining as reliable zero-cost baseline**: Prior work (CoCoHaNeRe, ACM TOSEM 2025) uses expensive gradient-based hard negative methods for code search. Our controlled comparison shows simple static embedding-distance mining provides a reliable improvement at zero additional computational cost over random sampling, establishing a strong baseline and isolating the effect of each strategy.\n2. **Secondary — Mechanistic explanation via representation geometry**: neg_difficulty analysis (margin distribution × strategy × encoder) offers preliminary explanatory depth beyond empirical comparison, offering a candidate heuristic (pos_sim threshold) that, if validated on more encoders, could help practitioners select strategies without exhaustive experiments.\n\n## Formal Definition\n\nGiven retrieval domain D, structural graph G, query q:\n**structure-grounded hard negative** = argmax_{x ∈ D : ¬reachable_G(x,q) ∧ x ∉ gold(q)} BM25(q, x)\n\nThe structural graph is externally fixed and does not update with the encoder (drift-immune). Negatives are selected from structurally unreachable files (i.e., x ∉ R_G(q), not connected via CodeQL data-flow to the query), ranked by BM25 (lexical similarity).\n\n## Title Options\n\n- Primary: \"What Makes Hard Negatives Work for Code Retrieval? A Systematic Study of Mining Strategies and Representation Geometry\"\n- Alternative: \"Static Embedding Negatives as a Zero-Cost Baseline for Contrastive Code Retrieval\" [contingent: activate only if M2 dataflow conditional benefit confirmed across encoders]\n- Backup: \"Simple Embedding Mining Suffices: A Zero-Cost Baseline for Hard Negative Mining in Code Retrieval\"\n\n## Venue Fit\n\n1. **EMNLP Main fit**: Empirical analysis + mechanistic insight papers are a strong EMNLP archetype (cf. \"Do Language Models...\" papers). Contribution is understanding, not just a new method.\n2. **NLP audience**: Frame as contrastive learning methodology with code as instantiation. Representation geometry analysis generalizes beyond code.\n3. **Against \"incremental\" criticism**: Paper demonstrates a reliable zero-cost baseline + offers preliminary mechanistic analysis of strategy × encoder interaction + validates on downstream task. Three contributions, not one marginal improvement.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n| Concern | Response | Evidence |\n|---------|----------|----------|\n| Only 2 encoders — can you generalize? | n=2 is minimum for interaction claim; we frame as observation with mechanism, not universal law; two decoder-only encoders with distinct pretraining (Fill vs FIM) and representation geometry (pos_sim 0.99 vs 0.80) provide meaningful variation | plan_013 (done), m2_full runs |\n| BM25 vs true embedding negatives? | Run BM25 negatives to empirically compare with embedding-distance mining, grounding the comparison against CoCoHaNeRe's gradient-based premise | plan_018 |\n| Pool size sensitivity? | Evaluate at pool {50, 100, 200, 500} to confirm strategy rankings hold at realistic sizes | plan_019 |\n| Stratified by structural data availability? | Report per-stratum R@1 for 34% with structural data vs 66% fallback | plan_005 (extended) |\n| Temperature regime specificity | Ablate τ ∈ {0.05, 0.1, 0.2} | plan_014 |\n| No downstream task evaluation | SWE-bench Lite end-to-end with LLM agent | plan_003 |\n| Static vs dynamic mining confound | Clarify mining is static; add dynamic baseline | plan_015 |\n| CodeQL scalability at larger scale | 0% timeout, 78% Z%, 123 repos; m1_reextract_v2 adds more | plan_006 (done) |\n| Why not compare inference-time graph methods? | Complementary: they navigate at inference, we mine at training. Discuss relationship. | plan_017 |\n| Single benchmark | Add RepoBench-R cross-file retrieval | plan_004 |\n| Statistical significance | Bootstrap 95% CI for all comparisons; full M2 = 9 seeds × 3 strategies × 2 encoders | plan_016, m2_full |\n| \"Complementary signal\" overstatement | Temper: \"conditionally beneficial\" not \"always complementary\" | Writing discipline |\n| Missing related work | RepoFusion, GRACE, GraphCodeBERT, ANCE, curriculum HN mining | Writing task |\n\n## Evidence Status\n\n### Confirmed findings (basis for claims):\n- D017: True embedding mining helps (pilot: +2.0pp Qwen, +1.6pp StarCoder; M2 seed 43: +0.52pp best_by_R@1, +0.82pp mean fixed-checkpoint; final range pending s42/s44 completion) — establishes reliable zero-cost baseline\n- D021/neg_difficulty: Qwen pos_sim=0.9855 (saturated), StarCoder pos_sim=0.7985 (healthy); margin differential is consistent with hypothesized strategy interaction\n- m2_embed_verify (pilot): 3-strategy comparison is consistent with pilot ranking (embedding > random, dataflow conditional)\n- plan_013 (StarCoder pilot): Consistent with cross-encoder pattern — embedding helps both, dataflow appears to help Qwen only\n- plan_006: CodeQL scalability confirmed at 123-repo scale\n\n### Evidence presentation strategy:\n- Primary table: best_by_R@1 (standard practice — select best checkpoint per run by validation R@1)\n- Supplementary: fixed-checkpoint comparison as robustness check (not a replacement for best_by_R@1)\n- Both metrics show directionally consistent results; no goalpost-moving between metrics\n\n### In progress:\n- m2_full_qwen: random s42 DONE (R@1=0.5527), s43 DONE (R@1=0.5554), s44 DONE (R@1=0.6009, early stopped step 500), embedding_distance_s43 DONE (R@1=0.5606, early stopped step 1500). 4/9 runs complete, ~35h remaining.\n- m2_full_starcoder: random s42 DONE (R@1=0.4604, from parent), s43 RUNNING (eval@500 R@1=0.4708). 1/9+ runs complete, ~65-70h remaining.\n- m1_reextract_v2: 90.5% complete (25,582/28,276), 0 ERROR, ETA ~19:00 CST May 15\n\n### Planned (priority order):\n- plan_018: BM25 negative baseline (Dream #1 consensus gap — grounds comparison against CoCoHaNeRe's premise)\n- plan_003: SWE-bench Lite eval (biggest lever for paper strength)\n- plan_019: Pool size sensitivity (Dream consensus — external validity)\n- plan_014: Temperature ablation (Dream reviewer concern)\n- plan_004: RepoBench-R second benchmark (with multi-seed CIs)\n- plan_015: Dynamic mining baseline\n- plan_005: Stratified analysis (extended: structural-available vs fallback stratum)\n- plan_007/008/009: Additional ablations\n\n## Limitations\n\nEffect size is smaller than pilot estimates, likely because full-scale training data provides stronger random baselines, reducing the marginal value of targeted negative selection.\n\n## Contingency (if full M2 results weaken claims)\n\n### All 18 runs confirm pilot pattern: Best case\n- Full statistical power, paper as framed above\n- Target: EMNLP Main\n\n### Dataflow benefit disappears even for Qwen at full scale: Pivot to 2-claim paper\n- Drop Claim 2 to analysis observation\n- Paper becomes: \"Simple embedding mining provides reliable zero-cost improvement + CodeQL extraction is practical + representation geometry analysis may help explain prior contradictions\"\n- Still publishable: reliable zero-cost baseline demonstration + mechanistic analysis is sufficient for EMNLP Main\n\n### BM25 negatives ≈ random (not harmful): Narrative pivot needed [D030 contingency]\n- If plan_018 shows BM25 negatives perform ≈ random (not << random), the narrative positioning needs adjustment\n- Pivot: \"systematic comparison reveals embedding-distance mining is the only strategy that consistently helps, establishing it as a reliable zero-cost baseline even when BM25 provides no signal\"\n- Paper remains valid as systematic analysis — the core finding (simple embedding mining provides reliable improvement) stands regardless of BM25 outcome\n- Claim 1 core preserved: \"Simple static embedding-distance mining provides a reliable retrieval improvement at zero additional computational cost over random sampling\"\n- Impact: BM25 result determines narrative nuance, not core contribution\n\n### Embedding mining also loses advantage at full scale: Major reassessment needed\n- Recheck data pipeline for bugs (full-scale data may have distribution differences)\n- If confirmed: negative result paper — \"Why Hard Negative Mining Doesn't Scale for Code Retrieval\"\n- Discuss with user before committing direction\n\n## Deprecated Claims (with reason)\n\n- ~~\"Embedding-distance hard negatives actively harm code retrieval\"~~ — FALSIFIED by D017. Original evidence (-27pp R@1) was from BM25-proxy negatives incorrectly labeled as embedding_distance in MVP. True embedding mining shows consistent improvement. Preserved here to prevent regression.\n\n<!-- Claim 2 wording fix: 2026-05-15 -->\n<!-- D041 dataflow-aware BM25 fix: 2026-05-15 -->\n<!-- D041 formal definition fix: reach